<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilbert Bill</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Criminal Procedure Code Amendment Act 1884</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ilbert Bill was a bill introduced to the Imperial Legislative Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in February 1883 that would have let senior Indian judges try cases involving white plaintiffs or defendants. Drafted by Sir Courtenay Ilbert and backed by the Governor-General, Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripon">Ripon</a>, it met fierce opposition from the European community, including plantation owners, whose protests and press drew on openly racist tropes. A compromise passed in January 1884 gave European defendants the right to a jury at least half European. The controversy strengthened Indian national awareness, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> was formed a year later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ilbert-bill.jpg" alt="Ilbert Bill" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sydney Prior Hall · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Support_for_the_Ilbert_Bill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
